diagnosis_text:
The fabric is healthy; no leaf–spine link or device fault is present. End-to-end data-plane works: pc_0_0 can reach pc_0_1 with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT in both directions (topology-wide reachability test). Control-plane is intact: both leafs have valid BGP-learned routes to the opposite host subnet (leaf_router_0_0 has B>* 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.4 on eth0; leaf_router_0_1 has B>* 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6 on eth0). Their uplink interfaces are up (leaf_router_0_0 eth0 172.16.0.5/31 and eth1 172.16.0.9/31 UP; leaf_router_0_1 eth0 172.16.0.7/31 and eth1 172.16.0.11/31 UP). 

On the spines, BGP sessions to the super-spine and both leaves are established and exchanging prefixes (spine_router_0_0 and spine_router_0_1 each show 3 neighbors up for ~00:01:42 with prefixes received). If a leaf–spine cable had failed, we would see BGP session drops, missing routes, or traffic loss, which is not observed.

Localization: No subsystem shows errors; BGP neighbors across all uplinks are Established, routes are present in FIB, and host-to-host ICMP succeeds.

Root cause: No current fault in the data-center fabric; the reported outage is not reproducible and was likely transient or host-side.

Corrective fix: None required on the fabric. Do not restart any device or link. Recommend the user re-test and, if issues recur, check pc_0_0/pc_0_1 host settings (NIC state, local firewall, ARP cache) and capture timestamps to correlate with fabric telemetry.
=== END ===